<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" dir="ltr" class="h-full bg-gray-100">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex">
    <title>Authorize an application — tectonic plates</title>
    <%== assets(:css) %>
    <script src="https://cdn.tailwindcss.com"></script>
  </head>
  <body>
    <main>
      <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4">
        <%# This page is reached from claude.ai or ChatGPT and from nowhere on this site, and
            one tap on it hands an API client the account, so the mark at the top is the whole
            of the answer to "am I really on Tectonic". A line of grey 14px text was the
            weakest answer available. It is deliberately not a link: the only ways out of this
            flow should be the two buttons at the bottom of the form. 224px wide on a phone and
            256px from 640px up, which reads as a brand mark rather than a favicon without
            becoming the page.

            Drawn into the page rather than fetched, which is what lets the wordmark be set in
            Blonde Sans; views/_logo.erb has the whole of that argument. The name is still
            reachable by anything that is not eyes, through the title the mark carries and the
            role that makes it an image, which is what the alt used to do. %>
        <%== render('_logo', locals: { classes: 'mx-auto mt-6 w-56 sm:w-64' }) %>
        <%== yield %>
      </div>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
